# CBP feed

`getBorderWaitTimes()` in `lib/border-wait-times.ts` is the only fetch of `https://bwt.cbp.gov/api/waittimes`. It uses `"use cache"` and `cacheLife("minutes")`. `cacheComponents` is enabled in `next.config.ts`. Do not add a second fetch or `unstable_cache`.

The raw body is a JSON array of snake_case port objects. Field names, the status strings CBP actually sends, and the normalized shape are in `docs/cbp-wait-times.md`. `normalizePort` drops an entry with no `port_number` or `port_name`. It also drops port `230106` (`B&M Bridge`), a second listing of Brownsville B&M that does not post a wait. Port `535501` is the row the app keeps. A non-array body or an empty list throws `BorderWaitTimesError`. A missing lane group becomes `EMPTY_LANE` (`operationalStatus: "N/A"`, counts `null`). `""` and `"N/A"` counts are `null`, not `0`.

`app/(app)/layout.tsx` calls `getBorderWaitTimes()` inside Suspense and passes the snapshot as `initial`. A thrown error becomes `initial={null}` so the client can retry. Do not fail the page.

`GET /api/wait-times` reuses that cached snapshot. `border` must be omitted, `mexican`, or `canadian` (400 otherwise). `port` matches port number, port name, or crossing name. A CBP failure or an empty feed returns 502. The response cache is `public, max-age=60, stale-while-revalidate=300`.

The wallet requests `/api/wait-times` with no query string. On-screen filtering happens in the browser.

`getLaneAverages()` in `lib/get-lane-averages.ts` reads `https://bwt.cbp.gov/api/bwtwaittimegraph/{bwtId}/{date}`. That is not a second fetch of `/api/waittimes`. The 8-digit `bwtId` is the crossings-list id whose last 6 digits are `port_number`. On the passenger graph, SENTRI and NEXUS are `fast_lane_*`. Pedestrian slots use CBP's `pedestrain` spelling. `GET /api/lane-averages?port=` returns 24 hourly averages per lane, plus the highest wait CBP lists for that hour (`high`), and uses `cacheLife("hours")`. An unknown crossing is 404. A CBP failure is 502.

The same response carries `typical`: for each lane, the median hourly average over the same weekday one to four weeks back (the graph URL takes a date, so these are four more fetches of the same endpoint, inside the same cache). An hour that fewer than two of those weeks published stays `null`, and a failed past week is skipped, not fatal. `outlookFor` in `lib/lane-averages.ts` is the only forecast: `live + (typical at the hour of arrival - typical now)`, never below 0, for 30 and 60 minutes out. The big number on a card is the 30 minute figure; the live CBP reading is the small number under it and "Right now" on the open card. Alerts, schedules, Ask, share text, and the link preview keep the live reading.
